{"name":"Chinese Hamster","hex":"#ebdbca","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hamster","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various Old World rodent species belonging to the subfamily Cricetinae.","In particular, a rodent of the species Mesocricetus auratus (the golden hamster) or of a species of the genera Cricetiscus and  Phodopus (the dwarf hamsters), often kept as a pet or used in scientific research."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To secrete or store privately, as a hamster does with food in its cheek pouches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hamster"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinese hamster","blocks":[{"text":"The Chinese hamster is a rodent in the genus Cricetulus of the subfamily Cricetidae that originated in the deserts of northern China and Mongolia. They are distinguished by an uncommonly long tail in comparison to other hamsters, most of whose tails are stubby. Chinese hamsters are primarily nocturnal; however, they will stay awake for brief periods, in between naps, throughout the day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Chinesedsaffa.jpg/330px-Chinesedsaffa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_hamster","wikidata":"Q2539773"},{"title":"Hamster","blocks":[{"text":"Hamsters are rodents belonging to the subfamily Cricetinae, which contains 19 species classified in seven genera. They have become established as popular small pets. The best-known species of hamster is the golden or Syrian hamster, which is the type most commonly kept as a pet. Other hamster species commonly kept as pets are the three species of dwarf hamster, Campbell's dwarf hamster, the winter white dwarf hamster and the Roborovski hamster, and the less common Chinese hamster."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/European_hamster_%28Cricetus_cricetus%29_Meidling.jpg/330px-European_hamster_%28Cricetus_cricetus%29_Meidling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamster","wikidata":"Q6573"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Normalchinese.JPG/120px-Normalchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Normalchinese.JPG/250px-Normalchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Normalchinese.JPG/120px-Normalchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANormalchinese.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Domspotchinese.JPG/120px-Domspotchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Domspotchinese.JPG/250px-Domspotchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Domspotchinese.JPG/120px-Domspotchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADomspotchinese.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/BEWchinese.JPG/120px-BEWchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/BEWchinese.JPG/250px-BEWchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/BEWchinese.JPG/120px-BEWchinese.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABEWchinese.JPG"},{"srcset":